1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Beneath the surface of the water

2
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
lies the heart of an operating nuclear reactor.

3
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The blue glow is made by gamma rays emitted from the source of radiation and is known as the Cherenkov effect.

4
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,000
This glow identifies the active core of a pool-type reactor.

5
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:51,000
One of four basic types of research reactors, versatile tools of nuclear exploration.

6
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Another is the tank-type research reactor, with the core buried deep inside a thick concrete shielding.

7
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:07,000
A third type, the aqueous homogeneous reactor, has a small steel vessel containing liquid

8
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:15,000
fuel deep inside a reflector of graphite blocks and a shield of concrete.

9
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The fourth is the graphite-moderated type, a research reactor in which the core itself

10
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
is a huge cube of graphite and fuel surrounded by concrete.

11
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Storage reactors of all types have in common the means of providing access to the core

12
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
for irradiating samples and conducting various experiments.

13
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Beam holes extending into areas of different neutron flux intensity, vertical irradiation

14
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:51,000
tubes, through tubes extending directly through the core itself, rabbit tubes operated pneumatically

15
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:57,000
or hydraulically, allowing rapid insertion and withdrawal of samples. Irradiation tunnels

16
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:04,000
for the exposure of large objects, including animals, to lower neutron fluxes beneath the

17
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
core. And thermal columns of graphite, where large volumes of thermal neutrons are easily

18
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:19,000
accessible. Throughout the world, research reactors of the types described, and modifications

19
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,000
and applications of these types are being used at various locations.

20
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:31,000
The operation of the first pool type reactor began at Oak Ridge National Laboratory in 1950.

21
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Since then this type has gained popularity because of its simplicity, flexibility, and moderate cost.

22
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:46,000
On the campus of the University of Michigan is an example of the pool reactor.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:50,000
The water in the pool is demineralized to remove impurities that would become

24
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
hazardously radioactive.

25
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
A depth of 22 feet above the core provides shielding.

26
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Down at the core level, the beam holds and through tubes

27
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:04,000
converge on the core to make use of the high neutron intensity,

28
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
about 10 to the 13th neutrons per square centimeter

29
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:12,000
per second when the reactor is operating at a thousand kilowatts.

30
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:15,000
The core of a pool reactor consists of a number of fuel elements

31
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:17,000
mounted vertically on a grid plate.

32
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Usually surrounded by reflector elements of beryllium or graphite

33
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:25,000
encased in aluminum.

34
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
Ion chambers and a fission chamber

35
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:32,000
are added to detect neutron activity within the core.

36
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Each fuel element consists of a number of thin aluminum-clad fuel plates,

37
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
spaced to permit free circulation of cooling water between them,

38
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
to carry off the heat.

39
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
Plates are like a sandwich, having enriched uranium as the meat

40
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
and aluminum as the bread.

41
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
The aluminum cladding protects the uranium from corrosion

42
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
and prevents the radioactive fission products from contaminating the water.

43
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:03,000
A few fuel elements have several plates removed

44
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:07,000
to provide a channel for inserting control rods.

45
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
and movable control rods can be introduced among the fuel elements.

46
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:17,000
A fuel loading consists of only 3 or 4 kilograms of enriched uranium

47
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
in a small volume compared with the many tons of natural uranium

48
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
in a graphite reactor.

49
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
The water serves as moderator, coolant,

50
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:28,000
and the major part of the shield.

51
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:31,000
The core is mounted on a frame and suspended in the water

52
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:34,000
from a motor-driven bridge across the pool.

53
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
The bridge carries much of the operating equipment,

54
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,000
including the safety and control rod drive assemblies.

55
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
The design of research facilities varies for different pool type reactors.

56
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,000
At the University of Michigan, when access to the core through other beam holes or the thermal column itself

57
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
is desired, the core is moved

58
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:02,000
out across the pool and into another position.

59
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
The far end of the pool is utilized for core storage and reloading.

60
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:13,000
Here also gamma ray irradiation work may be done with highly radioactive used fuel elements.

61
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:19,000
The instrumentation and operational controls are located in the control room overlooking the reactor.

62
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
A wall diagram enables the operator to keep track of the fuel elements,

63
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
which are moved to suit various experiments and to compensate for burn up of uranium.

64
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
Access to the thermal column is afforded from outside the pool at its base.

65
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Directly behind the sliding door is the graphite with several stringers that can

66
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
be readily removed for the insertion of samples to be irradiated by highly

67
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:53,000
thermalized neutrons. Pool-type reactors provide a substantial number of beam

68
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:00,000
holes, tubes, and other irradiation facilities for research purposes. This

69
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,000
reactor has 12 beam holes converging on the core and two large tubes that go

70
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:11,000
entirely through the reactor, through the high neutron flux close to the core.

71
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:15,000
There are also a number of pneumatic tubes

72
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
to provide convenient short-time exposure of samples.

73
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,000
During continuous operation at power levels above a few hundred kilowatts,

74
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:32,000
dissipation of the heat by natural convection becomes impractical.

75
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:43,000
The water in the pool has to be pumped from the plenum to A heat exchanger and the heat from the exchanger water dissipated from a cooling tower.

76
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:49,000
A portion of the returning pool water is continually circulated through a demineralizer to remove

77
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
dissolved minerals, which could become radioactive.

78
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,000
An open pool reactor can be designed to operate at a few megawatts.

79
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:05,000
However, the design is complicated by increased pool depth, thicker shielding, and by thermal

80
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:12,000
convections which bring enough radioactive nitrogen 16 to the surface to create a radiation hazard.

81
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,000
It becomes necessary to go to the tank-type reactor,

82
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:22,000
a compressed pool in effect, to facilitate handling the cooling water.

83
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:30,000
An example of this type is the Omega West reactor at Los Alamos scientific laboratory.

84
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:40,000
The pool is only a few feet in diameter. Ordinary water circulated by pumps acts as both coolant

85
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:46,000
and moderator. The top of the tank is sealed to allow slight pressurization to obtain sufficient

86
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:53,000
water flow through the core of the reactor. The operating mechanism for the control rods is on

87
00:07:53,000 --> 00:08:00,000
the top, also the access hole through which the fuel elements are replaced. The elements contain

88
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:08,000
enriched uranium in sandwich type plates. Several ports are available for access

89
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:16,000
to the core and the thermal column. In operation since early 1954 at the Argonne

90
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:22,000
National Laboratory, near Chicago, is a tank type research reactor using heavy

91
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:30,000
water. It is called the CP5. The reactor portion of the building is sealed off

92
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,000
from the rest of the structure by a double door airlock and maintained at slightly less

93
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,000
than atmospheric pressure.

94
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:45,000
This safety measure is to guard against the escape of radioactive gases to the environment

95
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
in the unlikely event of an accident.

96
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:54,000
The reactor uses enriched uranium as fuel and heavy water for the reflector, coolant

97
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:57,000
and moderator.

98
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:03,000
heavy water permits a design that provides more space for irradiation of samples with less fuel.

99
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Because of the long neutron diffusion length in heavy water, high neutron fluxes of over 10 to

100
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:17,000
the 13th at 2000 kilowatts are available in a relatively large volume for experimental use.

101
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:25,000
Graphite is used for a secondary reflector. The shielding consists of a thin boral liner

102
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:30,000
to absorb thermal neutrons and a lead liner to reduce gamma ray radiation.

103
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:36,000
It is backed by a high density concrete wall nearly five feet thick.

104
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:44,000
The tubular fuel elements contain enriched uranium clad with aluminum.

105
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:50,000
The cylindrical form provides access for fast neutron bombardment.

106
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:59,000
Under normal operation, only two kilograms of uranium-235 are required for a fuel charge,

107
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:07,000
which, because of fuel consumption, has to be replaced in about six months. Four cadmium safety

108
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:13,000
rods of the signal arm type move between the fuel elements to provide course control of the

109
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:21,000
chain reaction. Fine control is provided by one cadmium regulating rod operating

110
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:27,000
vertically near the core. A flow of heavy water circulates through a heat

111
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:34,000
exchanger and demineralizer and is returned. The radioactive nitrogen is

112
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:41,000
confined to this system. A gas handling system provides for recombining deuterium

113
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:47,000
and oxygen dissociated by radiation.

114
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:55,000
At the top of the reactor 45 vertical exposure holes are available for inserting and removing

115
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:58,000
samples and fuel elements.

116
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:03,000
The highly radioactive spent fuel elements are withdrawn into a lead coffin weighing

117
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:07,000
approximately eight tons and transferred to storage.

118
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:14,000
The CP5 reactor has two large thermal columns, providing variable space for irradiation and

119
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:20,000
other experimental work with slow neutrons.

120
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:25,000
For beam experiments, eight large beam holes are provided.

121
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:30,000
The neutron flux is high at the face of the reactor because high density concrete reduces

122
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:33,000
the shield thickness.

123
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:37,000
Two special isotope channels extend under the core.

124
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:42,000
Two large through tubes pass close to the core as do pneumatic tubes for quick exposure

125
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
of samples.

126
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:50,000
The argon CP5 reactor is designed primarily for neutron physics experiments.

127
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:58,000
But it has been found useful for producing isotopes and for some engineering test experiments.

128
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:04,000
A simple and relatively low-cost research reactor of low power but moderately high neutron flux

129
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:07,000
is the aqueous homogeneous or solution type.

130
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:16,000
The first reactor of this type was put into operation in 1943 at the Los Alamos Scientific Laboratory.

131
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:21,000
Enlarged twice, the present version has been in use since 1950.

132
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:29,000
In the city of Chicago, Illinois, another research reactor of the homogeneous type is

133
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:37,000
operated by the Armour Research Foundation and financed jointly by 24 industrial firms.

134
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:43,000
It is a simple and relatively low cost research reactor of low power, but fairly high neutron

135
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:48,000
flux, more than ten to the twelfth neutrons at 50 kilowatts.

136
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:53,000
The active core consists of enriched urinal sulfate dissolved in distilled water.

137
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:57,000
The water also serves as the moderator.

138
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:01,000
The solution is held in a stainless steel sphere.

139
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:07,000
Thus the enriched fuel and the water moderator are homogeneous, as contrasted with the lattice

140
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:11,000
arrangement of solid fuel in heterogeneous reactors.

141
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:17,000
A reflector of graphite completely surrounds the core.

142
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:24,000
rods enter thimbles built into the vessel. Water circulating in a coil of

143
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:29,000
stainless steel tubing removes the heat generated by fission and the fuel

144
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:36,000
solution. Leading from the core are other stainless steel pipes for handling the

145
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:41,000
fuel and the gases resulting from the fissioning process and from radiolysis

146
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:46,000
of the water. Above the core is an overflow assembly providing a space into

147
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:52,000
to which the liquid fuel is ejected, in case of a rapid power excursion resulting in a

148
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:59,000
reactor shutdown.

149
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:05,000
Neutron-absorbing control rods penetrate the sphere through the top.

150
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:11,000
They are actuated by electric motor drives and a housing on top of the shield.

151
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:16,000
To ensure against the accidental release of radioactivity to the surrounding area, this

152
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:26,000
reactor utilizes a steel core vessel, an aluminum envelope, and an airtight room as a triple

153
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,000
containment system.

154
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:37,000
A gas tight room containing the highly radioactive fission gas handling system provides an essentially

155
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:46,000
neutron-free gamma irradiation facility. A large graphite thermal column includes

156
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:52,000
several features which provide a variety of convenient exposure openings.

157
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:04,000
There are four vertical beam tubes penetrating the graphite reflector, two

158
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:11,000
two pneumatic tubes for rapid exposure of samples,

159
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:20,000
eight horizontal beam tubes that reach to the core,

160
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:25,000
as well as an exposure tube through the center of the core.

161
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:29,000
Instrumentation and controls are provided in a room above the reactor.

162
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:36,000
in all reactors, shutdown is easily accomplished by the use of the scram button. Because of its

163
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:44,000
low fuel inventory of only 1,300 grams, its high flux-to-power ratio, its neutron-free gamma

164
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:50,000
irradiation facility, and its compactness and relatively low cost, the solution-type reactor

165
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:58,000
is finding wide acceptance and utility. At the Brookhaven National Laboratory near New York City

166
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:02,000
is the United States' largest graphite research reactor.

167
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:06,000
The reactor built in 1942 by Enrico Fermi was of this type.

168
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:14,000
This multimillion-dollar reactor, at its full load power level of 30,000 kilowatts of heat,

169
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:19,000
produces a high neutron flux over 10 to the 12th

170
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:20,000
in a very large volume.

171
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:25,000
Behind the 5-foot wall of what we see simply as a concrete box

172
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:30,000
is the natural uranium fuel

173
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:35,000
in horizontal channels through a large mass of graphite moderator.

174
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:40,000
The core is a cube measuring about 25 feet on the side.

175
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:44,000
A neutron reflector of graphite surrounds the core.

176
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:49,000
Control rods operate in the fuel zone.

177
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:53,000
The heat produced in the core is removed by air

178
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:56,000
And finally, the reactor is enclosed in a thick concrete shield.

179
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:02,000
There are 700 tons of graphite in the 25-foot cube.

180
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:06,000
Through this run nearly 1,400 fuel channels.

181
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:13,000
In the original fuel element, 33 natural uranium slugs fit into an aluminum cartridge,

182
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:19,000
which was sealed to prevent corrosion and the escape of radioactive fission products.

183
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,000
The uranium slugs were encased in an aluminum cartridge with fins

184
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:27,000
to aid transfer of heat efficient to the cooling air.

185
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:32,000
Using these elements, the natural uranium loading was approximately 55 tons.

186
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:37,000
However, the natural uranium has been replaced with a new type fuel element

187
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:41,000
with enriched uranium and curved sandwich type plates of aluminum

188
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:45,000
requiring only about 60 kilograms of fuel for a loading.

189
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:54,000
The result, a four to five times increase in flux density without going to higher power.

190
00:17:54,000 --> 00:18:01,000
At full power, the temperature of this air leaving the reactor is 350 degrees Fahrenheit.

191
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:08,000
Five 1500 horsepower exhaust fans pull 750 tons of filtered air through the fuel channels

192
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:15,000
every hour at velocities approaching the speed of sound.

193
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:22,000
discharge it harmlessly to the atmosphere. The reactor is operated from a control room

194
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:30,000
which is manned around the clock. Safety is a primary consideration. Many independent

195
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:35,000
checkpoints automatically shut down the reactor if the power level goes too high, if fuel

196
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:42,000
temperature becomes excessive, or other abnormal operating conditions occur. A chief advantage

197
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:48,000
of the natural uranium-graphite reactor is the numerous openings and very large spaces

198
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:54,000
it provides for experimental purposes, with thermal neutron fluxes as high as 10 to the

199
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:58,000
13th, quite adequate for most purposes.

200
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:04,000
Coming out from the core are 30 experimental holes, which provide neutrons for experiments

201
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:07,000
either outside or inside the reactor.

202
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:12,000
11 pneumatic rabbit tubes penetrate the reactor to the center of the core

203
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:17,000
to provide very short-lived radioisotopes for immediate laboratory use

204
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:20,000
before much of the activity is lost.

205
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:26,000
On top of the reactor also are many large openings.

206
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:29,000
Although the capital investment is high,

207
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:35,000
the large irradiation space and natural uranium fuel offer many unique features.

208
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:41,000
Most research reactors clearly belong to one of these four basic types.

209
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:46,000
However, numerous adaptations are being tried, and new types may be developed.

210
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:53,000
At the Argonne National Laboratory is an adaptation of the tank type.

211
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:58,000
It is called the Argonaut, and is useful for some research,

212
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:02,000
but designed primarily for nuclear technology training.

213
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:10,000
At full power of a few kilowatts, it produces up to 10 to the 11th thermal neutrons per square centimeter per second.

214
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:17,000
Built into this low-cost, low-power reactor are many features of convenience, safety, and flexibility.

215
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:24,000
At one end is a thermal column, and at the other a movable tank for conducting shielding experiments.

216
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:29,000
The reactor design is so simple that laboratory people are urged to build it themselves.

217
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:36,000
The annular shape of the reactor vessel makes possible arrangements of aluminum clad fuel plates

218
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:45,000
in a ring around an internal graphite reflector. Approximately four kilograms of U-235 in 20%

219
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:50,000
material are used and plates can be put in different positions to meet the needs of the

220
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:56,000
experiment. Within the graphite center are five access holes. The outside of the tank

221
00:20:56,000 --> 00:21:02,000
is also surrounded by graphite, in which the control elements are located. Ordinary water

222
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:09,000
is pumped through the tank as both coolant and moderator. The instrumentation and controls

223
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:15,000
consist of easily procured standard devices. The uses of the argonaut in training include

224
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:21,000
start-up, criticality experiments and reactor operation, demonstrations in engineering and

225
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:28,000
nuclear physics and a variety of research experiments. Other very low

226
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:33,000
power reactors have been developed by industry. For example, solid homogeneous

227
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:38,000
reactors are being built to operate at a few watts to serve training and limited

228
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:43,000
research needs. They offer the unique feature of complete portability as well

229
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:49,000
as safety in operation. Its fuel consists of less than 700 grams of uranium-235

230
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:55,000
at 20 percent enrichment. Uranium oxide is mixed with solid polyethylene

231
00:21:55,000 --> 00:22:00,000
moderator and molded into disks which are housed in a graphite reflector and

232
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:07,000
shielded by lead and water. The small size, portability, and low cost make the

233
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:18,000
solid homogeneous reactor a practical classroom trainer. A low power solution

234
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:23,000
type homogeneous reactor with a flux of ten to the eighth has also been developed.

235
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:36,000
The reactor is fueled with about 1500 grams of uranium-235 in 20 percent material. It is dissolved

236
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:43,000
in 20 liters of uranyl sulfate in a small stainless steel sphere. This core surrounded

237
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:47,000
by a lead reflector shield is housed in an eight foot diameter tank of water.

238
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:53,000
This reactor is suitable for low flux research and training.

239
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:59,000
It can be shipped as a unit and its small size makes it practical for use in schools.

240
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:06,000
Research reactors are contributing to a continual program of experimentation and research,

241
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:13,000
probing and serving the fields of chemistry, physics, metallurgy, biology, and medicine.

242
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:16,000
The most familiar use is the production of radioisotopes.

243
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:26,000
Several hundred radioactive isotopes, one or more for nearly every element in the periodic table, are being employed in research, medicine, and industry.

244
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:31,000
For example, they are used as tags in tracer study of every description.

245
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
The measurement of fertilizer uptake in plants.

246
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:45,000
The diagnosis of thyroid carcinoma and other diseases by obtaining with the center scanner a plot of the localization of radioisotope deposit in the glands.

247
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:54,000
In the field of chemistry, radioisotopes trace the behavior of elements and compounds in chemical reactions.

248
00:23:55,000 --> 00:24:02,000
Special machines, using large gamma sources of cobalt-60 produced in high flux reactors,

249
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:05,000
are employed in the treatment of cancer and leukemia,

250
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:10,000
and in industry for making radioautographs of dense structure.

251
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:19,000
Radioisotopes are used in density gauges for various industrial purposes, such as continuously

252
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:24,000
measuring and controlling the thickness of a plastic sheet.

253
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:29,000
In addition to the production of radioisotopes, another major use of the research reactor

254
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:32,000
is in the study of basic physics.

255
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:40,000
Several experiments may be conducted simultaneously, such as the continuous cloud chamber experiment.

256
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:48,000
The neutron diffraction spectrometer experiment to study crystalline structure of elements and compound.

257
00:24:49,000 --> 00:24:59,000
The time of flight spectrometer, it employs a mechanical rotor or neutron chopper, a long flight tube, and a thousand channel analyzer.

258
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:05,000
This experiment measures nuclear cross-sections of isotopes.

259
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:12,000
For capture gamma-ray studies, the bent crystal spectrometer is used to cover

260
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:16,000
the gamma range from a few thousand to several million electron volts.

261
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:24,000
Irradiation of materials by fast neutrons produces changes in such mechanical properties as

262
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:30,000
tensile strength, creep and shear, and significant changes in electrical and thermal conductivity.

263
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:37,000
For effective engineering studies of changes in metals, samples must be irradiated to a total

264
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:44,000
exposure of about 10 to the 18th neutrons per square centimeter. Only with very high flux test

265
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:51,000
reactors can such irradiations be made in a reasonable time. In biology, mice and rats are

266
00:25:51,000 --> 00:26:00,000
utilized for radiation experiments. A study of the effect of radiation on bone marrow in immunity and

267
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:11,000
genealogy.

268
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:15,000
Reactors of low fluxes are used for activation analyses.

269
00:26:15,000 --> 00:26:20,000
A quantitative analysis can be made faster, and in some cases more accurately than by

270
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:27,000
conventional chemical means.

271
00:26:27,000 --> 00:26:34,000
fuel elements provide an intense source of pure gamma rays for food irradiation studies.

272
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:40,000
Pasteurization of foods and drugs allow for longer storage life. These are but a few of

273
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:46,000
the rapidly multiplying applications of research reactors. A practical program that utilizes

274
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:52,000
a research reactor involves a considerable financial investment. Detailed planning and

275
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:57,000
care must be exercised in the selection of the type of research reactor for a particular

276
00:26:57,000 --> 00:27:00,000
only a realistic objective review

277
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:04,000
and appraisal of the broad aims of an organization, its research

278
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:08,000
needs or training requirements can determine this selection

279
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:12,000
where research requires a reactor capable of producing

280
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:16,000
neutron fluxes in the range of 10 to the 12th to 10 to the

281
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:19,000
13th, the pool type will do the job

282
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:22,000
another is the homogeneous reactor

283
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:26,000
which also produces fluxes in this range, but in a smaller volume

284
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
and at a lower operating cost.

285
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:33,000
The tank type using heavy water

286
00:27:33,000 --> 00:27:37,000
provides a larger volume of irradiation space at these fluxes.

287
00:27:39,000 --> 00:27:43,000
The greater volume makes possible a large research program,

288
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:47,000
but the cost of heavy water must be justified by the research needs.

289
00:27:49,000 --> 00:27:54,000
The graphite-moderated type, with the same flux in a very large volume,

290
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:57,000
can support an extensive research program.

291
00:27:59,000 --> 00:28:07,000
Where the neutron fluxes required are substantially above the 10 to the 12th to 10 to the 13th range,

292
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:11,000
a reactor for materials or engineering testing is essential.

293
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:17,000
In limited research programs and for teaching purposes,

294
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
a simple low-flux reactor may satisfy the need.

295
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:28,000
The procurement of the reactor itself is not the only financial obligation imposed on a

296
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,000
research program.

297
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:36,000
There is also the need for associated equipment and facilities, manpower and supplies.

298
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:45,000
An entire scientific community, including chemical, physical, biological, metallurgical

299
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:50,000
in other laboratories, are all essential facilities.

300
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:55,000
Intensely radioactive specimens must be handled in hot cells equipped with

301
00:28:55,000 --> 00:29:00,000
intricate remote controlled devices. Heavy shields of dense concrete with

302
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:05,000
special shielding windows protect the operator while specimens are processed

303
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:13,000
and examined. A counting room is necessary, shielded to protect counting

304
00:29:13,000 --> 00:29:20,000
devices from outside radiation.

305
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:28,000
An electronic shop for the maintenance of sensitive controlling, measuring and

306
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:31,000
recording devices.

307
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:37,000
Personnel must be trained for the operation and maintenance of the reactor.

308
00:29:37,000 --> 00:29:40,000
All of these are important considerations

309
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:45,000
for the organization that undertakes a research program with a reactor.

310
00:29:45,000 --> 00:29:49,000
Wisely the functional requirements of the reactor will be kept close to the

311
00:29:49,000 --> 00:29:52,000
needs of the planned program.

312
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:56,000
The nuclear reactor is a powerful research tool

313
00:29:56,000 --> 00:30:05,000
offering to every branch of science new horizons in the service and needs of mankind.
